# An issue that adds a setting the core can't start without adds it, in the same pull request, to
# this file, to the core service in infra/deploy/compose.dev.yaml and to infra/deploy/.env.example
# (ADR-151). Otherwise it works from the IDE and fails in `just dev`. A secret's key also joins
# SECRET_HOLDERS in infra/deploy/check_dev_stack.py, and a key only the dev profile reads goes in
# infra/deploy/.env.example alone, never here (ADR-152).
#
# Set these as environment variables. Spring Boot does not read a .env file itself: export them,
# or pass a copy named .env to Docker with --env-file. .env is ignored by git; never commit values.
# The core connects as app, which owns no table (ADR-145). The migrate step reads the same keys set to
# dispatchflow_owner, from its own environment only: the owner's password never goes in this file.
SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL=
SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME=
SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=

# Every deployed environment also sets DISPATCHFLOW_DEPLOYED, with any value, so the core refuses to
# boot with the dev/test stand-in session there (ADR-147). The SMS provider's, the email sender's and
# the push service's adapters read their secrets from DISPATCHFLOW_SMS_CREDENTIAL,
# DISPATCHFLOW_EMAIL_CREDENTIAL and DISPATCHFLOW_PUSH_CREDENTIAL alone, which refuse it too. None has a
# line here: any of them set, even empty, stops a dev core from booting.
#
# Nor has DISPATCHFLOW_STANDIN_ACCOUNT, which only the dev and test profiles read: the Compose core
# and `just core-run` set it, and any other profile refuses to boot with it set (ADR-147).
